The [[International Criminal Tribunal for the Former Yugoslavia]] (ICTY) was a body of the [[United Nations]] established to prosecute serious crimes committed during the Yugoslav Wars, and to try their perpetrators. The tribunal was an [[ad hoc]] court located in [[The Hague]], [[Netherlands]]. One of the most prominent [[Trial of Slobodan Milošević|trials involved ex-Serbian President Slobodan Milošević]], who was in 2002 indicted on 66 counts of [[crimes against humanity]], [[war crimes]] and [[genocide]] allegedly committed in wars in Kosovo, Bosnia and Croatia.{{sfnp|Magliveras|2002|pp=661–677}} His trial remained incomplete since he died in 2006, before a verdict was reached.<ref>{{cite web|title=UN tribunal investigating death of accused genocide mastermind Slobodan Milosevic|url=https://news.un.org/en/story/2006/03/171822-un-tribunal-investigating-death-accused-genocide-mastermind-slobodan-milosevic|date=12 March 2006|publisher=UN News|access-date=4 May 2018|archive-date=4 May 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180504225313/https://news.un.org/en/story/2006/03/171822-un-tribunal-investigating-death-accused-genocide-mastermind-slobodan-milosevic|url-status=live}}</ref> Nonetheless, ICTY's trial "helped to delegitimize Milosevic's leadership", as one scholar put it.{{sfnp|Akhavan|2001|pp=7–31}}

Several convictions were handed over by the ICTY and its successor, the International Residual Mechanism for Criminal Tribunals (IRMCT). The first notable verdict confirming genocide in Srebrenica was the case against Serb General [[Radislav Krstić]]: he was sentenced in 2001, while the Appeals Chamber confirmed the verdict in 2004.<ref name="Krstic">{{cite web|title=Srebrenica massacre was genocide, UN tribunal for former Yugoslavia confirms|url=https://news.un.org/en/story/2004/04/100662-srebrenica-massacre-was-genocide-un-tribunal-former-yugoslavia-confirms|date=14 April 2004|publisher=UN News|access-date=7 January 2019|archive-date=7 January 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190107181205/https://news.un.org/en/story/2004/04/100662-srebrenica-massacre-was-genocide-un-tribunal-former-yugoslavia-confirms|url-status=live}}</ref> Another verdict was against ex-Bosnian Serb leader, [[Radovan Karadžić]], who was also convicted for genocide.<ref name="Karadzic">{{cite web|url=https://news.un.org/en/story/2016/03/525262-un-welcomes-historic-guilty-verdict-against-radovan-karadzic|date=24 March 2016|title=UN welcomes 'historic' guilty verdict against Radovan Karadžić|publisher=UN News|access-date=15 April 2018|archive-date=15 January 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190115023115/https://news.un.org/en/story/2016/03/525262-un-welcomes-historic-guilty-verdict-against-radovan-karadzic|url-status=dead}}</ref> On 22 November 2017, general [[Ratko Mladić]] was sentenced to a [[life in prison]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.un.org/apps/news/story.asp?NewsID=58143#.WhadpdLiXIU|title=UN hails conviction of Mladic, the 'epitome of evil,' a momentous victory for justice|date=22 November 2017|publisher=UN News|access-date=23 November 2017|archive-date=31 July 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210731100924/https://news.un.org/en/story/2017/11/636942-un-hails-conviction-mladic-epitome-evil-momentous-victory-justice#.WhadpdLiXIU|url-status=live}}</ref>

Other important convictions included those of ultranationalist [[Vojislav Šešelj]],<ref name="Seselj">{{cite web| url=http://www.unmict.org/en/news/appeals-chamber-reverses-%C5%A1e%C5%A1elj%E2%80%99s-acquittal-part-and-convicts-him-crimes-against-humanity| title=Appeals Chamber Reverses Šešelj's Acquittal, in Part, and Convicts Him of Crimes Against | work=United Nations International Residual Mechanism for Criminal Tribunals | date=11 April 2018| publisher=United Nations Mechanism for International Criminal Tribunals| access-date=11 April 2018| archive-date=12 April 2018| archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180412083601/http://www.unmict.org/en/news/appeals-chamber-reverses-%C5%A1e%C5%A1elj%E2%80%99s-acquittal-part-and-convicts-him-crimes-against-humanity| url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2018/04/serbia-conviction-of-war-criminal-delivers-long-overdue-justice-to-victims/|title=Serbia: Conviction of war criminal delivers long overdue justice to victims|date=11 April 2018|publisher=Amnesty International|access-date=11 April 2018|archive-date=12 April 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180412082203/https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2018/04/serbia-conviction-of-war-criminal-delivers-long-overdue-justice-to-victims/|url-status=live}}</ref> paramilitary leader [[Milan Lukić]],<ref>{{cite web|title=UN genocide tribunal affirms life sentence of Serb paramilitary leader|url=https://news.un.org/en/story/2012/12/427422-un-genocide-tribunal-affirms-life-sentence-serb-paramilitary-leader|date=4 December 2012|publisher=UN News|access-date=15 April 2018|archive-date=16 April 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180416200642/https://news.un.org/en/story/2012/12/427422-un-genocide-tribunal-affirms-life-sentence-serb-paramilitary-leader|url-status=live}}</ref> Bosnian Serb politician [[Momčilo Krajišnik]],<ref>{{cite web|title=UN tribunal transfers former Bosnian Serb leader to UK prison|url=https://news.un.org/en/story/2009/09/311692-un-tribunal-transfers-former-bosnian-serb-leader-uk-prison|date=8 September 2009|publisher=UN News|access-date=15 April 2018|archive-date=16 April 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180416200653/https://news.un.org/en/story/2009/09/311692-un-tribunal-transfers-former-bosnian-serb-leader-uk-prison|url-status=live}}</ref> Bosnian Serb general [[Stanislav Galić]], who was convicted for the [[siege of Sarajevo]],<ref name="Galic">{{cite web|title=UN war crimes tribunal sentences Bosnian Serb general to life in jail|url=https://news.un.org/en/story/2006/11/201412-un-war-crimes-tribunal-sentences-bosnian-serb-general-life-jail|date=30 November 2006|publisher=UN News|access-date=4 May 2018|archive-date=4 May 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180504225425/https://news.un.org/en/story/2006/11/201412-un-war-crimes-tribunal-sentences-bosnian-serb-general-life-jail|url-status=live}}</ref> the former Assistant Minister of the [[Serbian Ministry of Internal Affairs]] and Chief of its Public Security Department, [[Vlastimir Đorđević]], who was convicted for crimes in Kosovo,<ref>{{cite web|title=UN tribunal convicts former Serbian police official for crimes in Kosovo|url=https://news.un.org/en/story/2011/02/367392-un-tribunal-convicts-former-serbian-police-official-crimes-kosovo|date=23 February 2011|publisher=UN News|access-date=4 May 2018|archive-date=4 May 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180504225234/https://news.un.org/en/story/2011/02/367392-un-tribunal-convicts-former-serbian-police-official-crimes-kosovo|url-status=live}}</ref> ex-JNA commander [[Mile Mrkšić]]<ref>{{cite news|title=Mile Mrksic, a Serb Army Officer Convicted of War Crimes, Dies at 68|work=[[The New York Times]]|date=17 August 2015|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/18/world/europe/mile-mrksic-a-serb-army-officer-convicted-of-war-crimes-dies-at-68.html|access-date=15 April 2018|archive-date=16 April 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180416203649/https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/18/world/europe/mile-mrksic-a-serb-army-officer-convicted-of-war-crimes-dies-at-68.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=UN war crimes tribunal sentences two former senior Yugoslav officers|url=https://news.un.org/en/story/2007/09/233322-un-war-crimes-tribunal-sentences-two-former-senior-yugoslav-officers|date=27 September 2007|publisher=UN News|access-date=4 May 2018|archive-date=4 May 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180504225411/https://news.un.org/en/story/2007/09/233322-un-war-crimes-tribunal-sentences-two-former-senior-yugoslav-officers|url-status=live}}</ref> as well as both of [[Republic of Serbian Krajina]] ex-Presidents [[Milan Martić]]<ref>{{cite web|title=UN tribunal upholds 35-year jail term for leader of breakaway Croatian Serb state|url=https://news.un.org/en/story/2008/10/277112-un-tribunal-upholds-35-year-jail-term-leader-breakaway-croatian-serb-state|date=8 October 2008|publisher=UN News|access-date=15 April 2018|archive-date=17 April 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180417022756/https://news.un.org/en/story/2008/10/277112-un-tribunal-upholds-35-year-jail-term-leader-breakaway-croatian-serb-state|url-status=live}}</ref> and [[Milan Babić]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Convicted Croatian Serb ex-leader commits suicide before he was to testify at UN court|url=https://news.un.org/en/story/2006/03/171232-convicted-croatian-serb-ex-leader-commits-suicide-he-was-testify-un-court|date=6 March 2006|publisher=UN News|access-date=7 January 2019|archive-date=7 January 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190107181217/https://news.un.org/en/story/2006/03/171232-convicted-croatian-serb-ex-leader-commits-suicide-he-was-testify-un-court|url-status=live}}</ref> The follow-up [[International Residual Mechanism for Criminal Tribunals]] convicted [[Jovica Stanišić]] and [[Franko Simatović]], [[State Security Service (FR Yugoslavia)|State Security Service]] officers within the [[Ministry of Internal Affairs (Serbia)|Ministry of Internal Affairs of Serbia]], and concluded:

{{blockquote|The Trial Chamber emphasized that the crimes were not committed in a random or disorganized manner, but rather during the course of well-planned and coordinated operations, demonstrating the existence of a common criminal purpose. In this respect, it emphasized the systematic pattern of crimes committed against non-Serb civilians in all regions covered by the Indictment. In this context, the Trial Chamber concluded that these crimes formed part of the [[Joint criminal enterprise|common criminal purpose]] to forcibly and permanently remove non-Serbs from large areas of [[Croatia]] and [[Bosnia and Herzegovina]] through the crimes of [[persecution]], murder, deportation, and inhumane acts ([[Forced displacement|forcible transfer]]) charged in the Indictment. It further found that this common purpose was shared by senior political and military officials, including [[Slobodan Milošević]], [[Radmilo Bogdanović]], [[Radovan Stojičić]] (Badža), [[Mihalj Kertes]], Milan Martić, Milan Babić, [[Goran Hadžić]], Radovan Karadžić, Ratko Mladić, Momčilo Krajišnik, [[Biljana Plavšić]], and [[Arkan|Željko Ražnatović (Arkan)]].<ref>{{cite web| title=The Prosecutor vs. Jovica Stanišić & Franko Simatović – Judgement In the Appeals Chamber| url=https://www.irmct.org/sites/default/files/case_documents/IRMCT-Appeal-Judgement-Stanisic-Simatovic-ENG.pdf |page=209| publisher=International Residual Mechanism for Criminal Tribunals| location=The Hague| date=31 May 2023| access-date=17 August 2023}}</ref>}}

The ICTY also declared about the political goals of the war:
{{blockquote|The political goals of the Serbian authorities in Belgrade appear to have been to carve a new set of territories for the Serbs out of both Croatia and Bosnia and Herzegovina, to be added to Serbia and Montenegro. These coincided with the attempts of the JNA forces to prevent each of the Republics from achieving effective independence.<ref>[[#ICTY-Delalic|''Prosecutor v. Delalić et al. – Judgement'', 16 November 1998]], p. 46</ref>}}

Several Croats, Bosniaks and Albanians were convicted for crimes, as well, including ex-Herzegovina Croat leader [[Jadranko Prlić]] and commander [[Slobodan Praljak]],<ref name="Prlic">{{cite web| title=Prlić et al., Case Information Sheet| publisher=International Criminal Tribunal for the Former Yugoslavia| url=http://www.icty.org/x/cases/prlic/cis/en/cis_prlic_al_en.pdf| access-date=14 January 2019| archive-date=15 September 2011| archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110915160300/http://www.icty.org/x/cases/prlic/cis/en/cis_prlic_al_en.pdf| url-status=live}}</ref> Bosnian Croat military commander [[Mladen Naletilić]],<ref>{{cite web|title=Bosnian Croat commander convicted by UN tribunal to serve jail term in Italy|url=https://news.un.org/en/story/2008/04/257282-bosnian-croat-commander-convicted-un-tribunal-serve-jail-term-italy|date=25 April 2008|publisher=UN News|access-date=4 May 2018|archive-date=28 September 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210928194440/https://news.un.org/en/story/2008/04/257282-bosnian-croat-commander-convicted-un-tribunal-serve-jail-term-italy|url-status=live}}</ref> ex-Bosnian Army commander [[Enver Hadžihasanović]]<ref>{{cite web|title=UN tribunal partially overturns convictions of two Bosnian Muslim commanders|url=https://news.un.org/en/story/2008/04/256912-un-tribunal-partially-overturns-convictions-two-bosnian-muslim-commanders|date=22 April 2008|publisher=UN News|access-date=4 May 2018|archive-date=4 May 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180504225245/https://news.un.org/en/story/2008/04/256912-un-tribunal-partially-overturns-convictions-two-bosnian-muslim-commanders|url-status=live}}</ref> and ex-Kosovo commander [[Haradin Bala]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Kosovo prison guard convicted by UN tribunal to serve rest of jail term in France|url=https://news.un.org/en/story/2008/05/259432-kosovo-prison-guard-convicted-un-tribunal-serve-rest-jail-term-france|publisher=UN News|access-date=15 April 2018|date=2008-05-15|archive-date=16 April 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180416200630/https://news.un.org/en/story/2008/05/259432-kosovo-prison-guard-convicted-un-tribunal-serve-rest-jail-term-france|url-status=live}}</ref>

In the [[Trial of Gotovina et al.|Trial of Gotovina et al]], Croatian Generals [[Ante Gotovina]] and [[Mladen Markač]] were ultimately acquitted on appeal in 2012.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.icty.org/x/cases/gotovina/acjug/en/121116_summary.pdf|publisher=[[International Criminal Tribunal for the former Yugoslavia]]|title=Appeals Judgement Summary for Ante Gotovina and Mladen Markač|date=16 November 2012|access-date=12 January 2013|archive-date=18 December 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201218133450/https://www.icty.org/x/cases/gotovina/acjug/en/121116_summary.pdf|url-status=live}}</ref>

By 2019, based on its statute,<ref>{{cite web| url=http://www.icty.org/x/file/Legal%20Library/Statute/statute_sept09_en.pdf| publisher=International Criminal Tribunal for the Former Yugoslavia| title=Updated Statute of the International Criminal Tribunal for the Former Yugoslavia| date=September 2009| access-date=14 January 2019| archive-date=21 March 2019| archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190321175735/http://www.icty.org/x/file/Legal%20Library/Statute/statute_sept09_en.pdf| url-status=live}}</ref> the ICTY concluded that the Serb officials were found guilty of [[persecutions]], [[deportation]] and/or [[forcible transfer]] ([[crimes against humanity]], Article 5) in Croatia,<ref name="Martic">{{cite web|title=Leader of breakaway Croatian Serb state convicted and jailed by UN tribunal|url=https://news.un.org/en/story/2007/06/222002-leader-breakaway-croatian-serb-state-convicted-and-jailed-un-tribunal|date=12 June 2007|publisher=UN News|access-date=15 April 2018|archive-date=20 April 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180420073822/https://news.un.org/en/story/2007/06/222002-leader-breakaway-croatian-serb-state-convicted-and-jailed-un-tribunal|url-status=live}}</ref> Bosnia and Herzegovina,<ref name="Karadzic"/> Kosovo<ref name="Sainovic">{{cite web| title=Šainović et al., Case Information Sheet| url=http://www.icty.org/x/cases/milutinovic/cis/en/cis_sainovic_al_en.pdf| publisher=International Criminal Tribunal for the Former Yugoslavia| date=2014| access-date=11 January 2019| archive-date=22 January 2020| archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200122070440/https://www.icty.org/x/cases/milutinovic/cis/en/cis_sainovic_al_en.pdf| url-status=live}}</ref> and [[Vojvodina]].<ref name="Seselj"/> They were also found guilty of [[murder]] (crimes against humanity, Article 5) in Croatia,<ref name="Martic"/> Bosnia and Herzegovina<ref name="Karadzic"/> and Kosovo;<ref name="Sainovic"/> as well as [[Terror (politics)|terror]] (violations of [[Law of war|the laws or customs of war]], Article 3)<ref name="Galic"/> and genocide (Article 4)<ref name="Krstic"/><ref name="Karadzic"/> in Bosnia and Herzegovina. The Croat forces were not found guilty of anything in Croatia, but were found guilty of deportation, other inhumane acts (forcible transfer), murder and persecutions (crimes against humanity, Article 5) in Bosnia and Herzegovina.<ref name="Prlic"/> The Bosniak forces were found guilty of inhuman treatment (grave breaches of the [[Geneva Conventions]], Article 2), murder; cruel treatment (violations of the laws or customs of war, Article 3) in Bosnia and Herzegovina.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.icty.org/x/cases/mucic/cis/en/cis_mucic_al_en.pdf|publisher=International Criminal Tribunal for the Former Yugoslavia|title=Mucić et al., Case Information Sheet|access-date=14 January 2019|archive-date=26 November 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211126033456/https://www.icty.org/x/cases/mucic/cis/en/cis_mucic_al_en.pdf|url-status=live}}</ref> One Albanian official was found guilty of torture, cruel treatment, murder (violations of the laws or customs of war, Article 3) in Kosovo.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.icty.org/x/cases/limaj/cis/en/cis_limaj_al_en.pdf|publisher=International Criminal Tribunal for the Former Yugoslavia|title=Limaj et al., Case Information Sheet|access-date=14 January 2019|archive-date=27 August 2010|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100827083654/http://www.icty.org/x/cases/limaj/cis/en/cis_limaj_al_en.pdf|url-status=live}}</ref>

==Aftermath==
=== Gunrunning ===
After the fighting ended, millions of weapons were left with civilians who held on to them in case violence should resurface. These weapons later turned up on the arms black market of Europe.<ref name="ti_23Nov2015">{{cite news|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/europe/paris-terror-attack-why-getting-hold-of-a-kalashnikov-is-so-easy-a6746026.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220507/https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/europe/paris-terror-attack-why-getting-hold-of-a-kalashnikov-is-so-easy-a6746026.html |archive-date=2022-05-07 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live|title=Paris terror attack: Why getting hold of a Kalashnikov is so easy|date=23 November 2015|work=[[The Independent]]|access-date=28 November 2015}}{{cbignore}}</ref>

In 2018, there were no exact official figures on how many firearms are missing; in Serbia, authorities have given estimates which range from 250,000 to 900,000, different kinds of firearms are in circulation. In Bosnia, public reports contain a figure of 750,000. At the end of 2017, a man entered a bus in Banja Luka carrying two bags with 36 hand grenades, three assault rifles, seven handguns, a mine and hundreds of cartridges with Gothenburg as the destination. He was stopped in the neighbouring country of Slovenia. A 26-year-old woman was stopped at the border to Croatia with three antitank weapons and a hand grenade. Police found four machine guns, three battle rifles, three assault rifles and a large quantity of explosives at the home of a 79-year-old man. According to a UNDP official, getting civilians to give up their arms to state authorities is complicated as people are then forced to trust that authorities will protect them. Instead, criminals collect the weapons.<ref name=":0">{{cite web|url=http://www.gp.se/1.11576453|title=GP granskar: Vapnens väg till Göteborg|website=gp.se|date=7 December 2018|language=sv|access-date=2018-12-10|archive-date=8 February 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230208124120/https://www.gp.se/nyheter/g%C3%B6teborg/gp-granskar-vapnens-v%C3%A4g-till-g%C3%B6teborg-1.11576453|url-status=live}}</ref> Some of the missing weapons were used in the [[November 2015 Paris attacks]] during which 130 people were killed by [[Jihadism|jihadists]]. Other arms were assault rifles which were used in the [[2015 Gothenburg pub shooting]].<ref name=":0" />

Successor-state governments' efforts to reduce the prevalence of illegally held arms are co-ordinated through a Regional Approach to Stockpile Reduction (RASR) focused on reducing stockpiles, arms diversion and unexplained explosions in South-east Europe. Partners include the European Union, the US Office of Weapons Removal and Abatement, the US Defense Threat Reduction Agency (DTRA) and NATO's Support and Procurement Agency.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.rasrinitiative.org/partners.php|title=Regional Approach to Stockpile Reduction – RASR – Partners|website=www.rasrinitiative.org|access-date=2019-02-16|archive-date=1 December 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221201083242/https://www.rasrinitiative.org/partners.php|url-status=live}}</ref> Funded by the US Government, activities include annual workshops attended by US government officials from the Departments of State and Defense and defense ministry representatives from the Yugoslav successor states.<ref name=":02">{{cite book|title=Small arms survey 2015: weapons and the world |isbn=978-1-107-32363-6 |publisher=Cambridge University Press |oclc=913568550 |author=Small Arms Survey |author-link=Small Arms Survey |date=July 2015 |page=128}}</ref>

=== Drug trafficking ===
Since the beginning of hostilities between warring factions in the former [[Yugoslavia]], the [[Kosovo Liberation Army]] as well as the [[Serbian mafia]] have been involved in the [[illegal drug trade]], particularly with [[heroin|West Asian heroin]] entering Central and [[Western Europe]]. In the early 1990s, 2,000 Albanians from [[Kosovo]] were held in Swiss jails on charges of arms and drug smuggling. Over the course of the war, a total of several tons of heroin were confiscated by [[Interpol]] and local law enforcement. Illegal drug smuggling operations also led to additional crimes all across Western Europe, which included bank robberies and extortion committed by criminal gangs operating out of [[Eastern Europe]]. The intensification of heroin consumption in Western Europe led to the expansion of open air drug markets, particularly in [[Switzerland]]. Bosnian criminal gangs continue to have a significant impact on global drug trafficking, through entering the lucrative [[cocaine trade]].<ref>Drozdiak, William (November 6, 1993). [https://www.washingtonpost.com/archive/politics/1993/11/06/balkan-war-victor-heroin/ae8f3ea1-1fd2-4bf5-90c1-48f7ecd803c3/ "Balkan War Victor: Heroin"]. ''The Washington Post''. Retrieved 3 May 2021.</ref><ref>[https://www.theguardian.com/world/2010/dec/14/kosovo-prime-minister-llike-mafia-boss "Kosovo PM is head of human organ and arms ring, Council of Europe reports"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221219104403/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2010/dec/14/kosovo-prime-minister-llike-mafia-boss |date=19 December 2022 }}. ''theguardian.com''. Retrieved 3 May 2021.</ref><ref>[https://www.europol.europa.eu/newsroom/news/balkan-cartel-trafficking-cocaine-around-globe-in-private-planes-busted "Balkan Cartel Trafficking Cocaine Around the Globe in Private Planes Busted"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211024054906/https://www.europol.europa.eu/newsroom/news/balkan-cartel-trafficking-cocaine-around-globe-in-private-planes-busted |date=24 October 2021 }}. ''Europol''. Retrieved 3 May 2021.</ref>

=== Free trade ===
In 2006 the Central European Free Trade Agreement ([[Central European Free Trade Agreement|CEFTA]]) was expanded to include many of the previous Yugoslav republics, in order to show that despite the political conflicts economic cooperation was still possible. CEFTA went into full effect by the end of 2007.<ref>{{Cite web |title=MEI - The agreement on free trade in the Balkans (cefta) |url=https://www.mei.gov.rs/eng/documents/agreements-with-the-eu/the-agreement-on-free-trade-in-the-balkans-cefta/ |access-date=2024-10-14 |website=www.mei.gov.rs |language=en}}</ref>

==Timeline==
{{For timeline|Timeline of the Yugoslav Wars}}

'''1990'''
*[[Log Revolution]]. [[SAO Krajina]] is proclaimed over an indefinite area of Croatia.

'''1991'''
[[File:Balkans War 1991, Latest deaths - Flickr - Peter Denton 丕特 . 天登.jpg|thumb|People observing new death notifications on a wall in [[Dubrovnik]] during the siege, December 1991]]
*Slovenia and Croatia declare independence in June, North Macedonia in September. [[Ten-Day War|War in Slovenia]] lasts ten days, and results in dozens of fatalities. The Yugoslav army leaves Slovenia after intervention of the UN which insisted that Slovenia be allowed to leave, but supports rebel Serb forces in Croatia. The [[Croatian War of Independence]] begins in Croatia. Serb areas in Croatia declare independence, but are recognized only by Belgrade.
*[[Vukovar]] is devastated by bombardments and shelling, and other cities such as [[Dubrovnik]], [[Karlovac]] and [[Osijek]] sustain extensive damage.{{sfnp|Zaknic|1992|pp=115–124}} Refugees from war zones overwhelm Croatia, while Europe is slow to accept refugees.
*In Croatia, about 250,000 Croats and other non-Serbs forced from their homes or fled the violence.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.hrw.org/en/news/2001/10/28/milosevic-important-new-charges-croatia|title=Milosevic: Important New Charges on Croatia|access-date=29 October 2010|date=21 October 2001|work=Human Rights Watch|location=The Hague|archive-date=25 December 2010|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101225134329/http://www.hrw.org/en/news/2001/10/28/milosevic-important-new-charges-croatia|url-status=live}}</ref>

'''1992'''
[[File:Sarajevo Siege firewood couple 1992.jpg|thumb|Besieged residents collect firewood in the bitter winter of 1992 during the [[Siege of Sarajevo]].]]
*[[Cyrus Vance|Vance Plan]] signed, creating four [[United Nations Protection Force]] zones for Serbs and ending large-scale fighting in Croatia.
*Bosnia declares independence. [[Bosnian war]] begins with the Bosnian Serb military leadership, most notably [[Ratko Mladić]], trying to create a new, separate Serb state, [[Republika Srpska]], through which they would conquer as much of Bosnia as possible for the vision of either a Greater Serbia{{sfnp|Off|2010|p=218}} or a [[rump state|rump]] Yugoslavia.<ref>{{Cite news|last=Sudetic|first=Chuck|date=1991-10-24|title=Top Serb Leaders Back Proposal To Form Separate Yugoslav State|language=en-US|work=The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1991/10/24/world/top-serb-leaders-back-proposal-to-form-separate-yugoslav-state.html|access-date=2020-05-28|issn=0362-4331|archive-date=22 September 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220922022718/https://www.nytimes.com/1991/10/24/world/top-serb-leaders-back-proposal-to-form-separate-yugoslav-state.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book|last=Pinder|first=David|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=Nyq5AAAAIAAJ|title=The New Europe: Economy, Society and Environment|date=1998|publisher=Wiley|page=34|isbn=978-0-471-97123-8|language=en|access-date=8 February 2023|archive-date=8 February 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230208124141/https://books.google.com/books?id=Nyq5AAAAIAAJ|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book|last=Crnobrnja|first=Mihailo|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=zZnagDP3gO4C&q=The+Yugoslav+Drama|title=The Yugoslav Drama|date=1996|publisher=McGill-Queen's Press – MQUP|page=176|isbn=978-0-7735-1429-4|language=en|access-date=8 February 2023|archive-date=8 February 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230208124123/https://books.google.com/books?id=zZnagDP3gO4C&q=The+Yugoslav+Drama|url-status=live}}</ref>
*[[Federal Republic of Yugoslavia]] proclaimed, consisting of [[Serbia]] and [[Montenegro]], the two remaining republics.
*United Nations impose [[Sanctions against Yugoslavia|sanctions against FR Yugoslavia]] for its support of the unrecognized [[Republic of Serbian Krajina]] in Croatia and [[Republika Srpska]] in Bosnia.<ref>{{cite news | title = Angry U.N. Votes Harsh Sanctions on Yugoslavia : Balkans: The Security Council, infuriated by bloody attacks in Bosnia-Herzegovina, imposes an oil embargo and other curbs. China, Zimbabwe abstain in 13–0 vote | date = 31 May 1992 | newspaper = LA Times | url = https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-1992-05-31-mn-1673-story.html | author = Stanley Meisler and Carol J. Williams | access-date = 7 August 2017 | archive-date = 11 August 2018 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20180811160816/http://articles.latimes.com/1992-05-31/news/mn-1673_1_security-council | url-status = live }}</ref> In May 1992, Slovenia, Croatia and Bosnia become UN members. FR Yugoslavia claims being sole legal heir to SFRY, which is disputed by other republics. UN envoys agree that Yugoslavia had 'dissolved into constituent republics'.
*The Yugoslav army retreats from Bosnia, but leaves its weapons to the army of Republika Srpska, which attacks poorly armed Bosnian cities of Zvornik, Kotor Varoš, Prijedor, Foča, [[Višegrad (municipality)|Višegrad]], Doboj. [[Prijedor ethnic cleansing]] and [[siege of Sarajevo]] start. Hundreds of thousands of non-Serbian refugees flee.
*[[Croat–Bosniak War|Bosniak-Croat conflict]] begins in Bosnia.

'''1993'''
[[File:HVO Army T-55 Glamoc setup.jpg|thumb|Two [[Croatian Defense Council]] (HVO) [[T-54/55|T-55]] main battle tanks pull into firing position during a three-day exercise held at the Barbara Range in [[Glamoč]], [[Republic of Bosnia and Herzegovina|Bosnia and Herzegovina]].]]
[[File:SrebrenicaStone.jpg|thumb|[[Srebrenica Genocide Memorial]] Stone at [[Donji Potočari|Potočari]]]]
*[[Siege of Bihać (1992–95)|Fighting begins in the Bihać]] region between Bosnian Government forces loyal to [[Alija Izetbegović]], and Bosniaks loyal to [[Fikret Abdić]], also supported by the Serbs.
*Sanctions in FR Yugoslavia, now isolated, create hyperinflation of 300&nbsp;million percent of the Yugoslav dinar.<ref name="YUGOSLAV ECONOMY FORECAST TO GROW ONCE EMBARGO ENDS INFLATION WHIPPED"/> 
*[[Ahmići massacre]]: the Croat forces kill over a hundred Bosnian Muslims.
*[[Siege of Mostar|Battle of Mostar]]. [[UNESCO World Heritage Site]] [[Stari Most]] (The Old Bridge) in [[Mostar]], built in 1566, was destroyed by Croatian [[Croatian Defense Council|HVO]] forces.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/croats-destroy-mostars-historic-bridge-1503338.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220507/https://www.independent.co.uk/news/croats-destroy-mostars-historic-bridge-1503338.html |archive-date=2022-05-07 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live | title = Croats destroy Mostar's historic bridge | first1 = Robert | last1 = Block | first2 = Christopher | last2 = Bellamy | date = 10 November 1993 | work = The Independent}}{{cbignore}}</ref> It was rebuilt in 2003.
*[[Army of the Republic of Bosnia and Herzegovina|ARBiH]] launch [[Operation Neretva '93]] against [[Croatian Defence Council|HVO]] in [[Herzegovina]] which ended in a stalemate.

'''1994'''
*[[Markale massacres|Markale market shelling]] in Sarajevo.
*[[Washington Agreement|Peace treaty]] between Bosniaks and Croats arbitrated by the United States, [[Federation of Bosnia and Herzegovina]] formed.
*FR Yugoslavia starts slowly suspending its financial and military support for Republika Srpska.<ref>{{cite news | title=Serbia Cuts Off Bosnian Rebels : Balkans: Belgrade, under international pressure, says it is denying supplies of fuel and arms to forces it has supported. Washington cautiously welcomes move | url=https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-1994-08-05-mn-23753-story.html | date=5 August 1994 | author=Carol J. Williams | access-date=7 July 2017 | newspaper=LA Times | archive-date=6 March 2016 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160306182933/http://articles.latimes.com/1994-08-05/news/mn-23753_1_bosnian-serbs | url-status=live }}</ref>

'''1995'''
*[[Srebrenica massacre]] reported. 8,000 Bosniaks killed by Serb forces.<ref name="Radovan Karadzic guilty of genocide over Srebrenica massacre in Bosnia" />
*Croatia launches [[Operation Flash]], recapturing a part of its territory, but tens of thousands of Serb civilians flee from the area. The RSK responds with the [[Zagreb rocket attack]].
*Croatia launches [[Operation Storm]], reclaiming all UNPA zones except Eastern Slavonia, and resulting in exodus of 150,000–200,000 Serbs from the zones. Yugoslav forces do not intervene. War in Croatia ends.
*[[1995 NATO bombing in Bosnia and Herzegovina|NATO launches a series of air strikes]] on Bosnian Serb artillery and other military targets. Croatian and Bosnian army start a joint offensive against [[Republika Srpska]].
*[[Dayton Agreement]] signed in Paris. War in Bosnia and Herzegovina ends. Aftermath of war is over 100,000 killed and missing and two million people internally displaced or refugees.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.unhcr.org/cgi-bin/texis/vtx/search?page=search&docid=414ffeb44&query=bosnia|work=UNHCR, The UN Refugee Agency|publisher=United Nations High Commissioner for Refugees|title=Returns to Bosnia and Herzegovina reach 1 million: This is a summary of what was said by UNHCR spokesperson Ron Redmond – to whom quoted text may be attributed – at today's press briefing at the Palais des Nations in Geneva.|access-date=11 August 2012|date=21 September 2004|archive-date=22 May 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130522181745/http://www.unhcr.org/cgi-bin/texis/vtx/search?page=search&docid=414ffeb44&query=bosnia|url-status=live}}</ref>

'''1996'''
*FR Yugoslavia recognizes Croatia and Bosnia & Herzegovina.
*Fighting breaks out in Kosovo between Albanians rebels and FR Yugoslav authorities.
*Following allegations of fraud in local elections, tens of thousands of Serbs demonstrate in Belgrade against the Milošević government for three months.<ref>{{cite news | title=20,000 Attend a Protest Against Serbian Leader | url=https://www.nytimes.com/1996/03/10/world/world-news-briefs-20000-attend-a-protest-against-serbian-leader.html | work=The New York Times | date=10 March 1996 | access-date=7 July 2017 | archive-date=18 January 2018 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180118010543/http://www.nytimes.com/1996/03/10/world/world-news-briefs-20000-attend-a-protest-against-serbian-leader.html | url-status=live }}</ref>

'''1998'''
*[[UNTAES|Eastern Slavonia]] peacefully reintegrated into Croatia, following a gradual three-year handover of power.
*Fighting in Kosovo gradually escalates between Albanians demanding independence and the state.

'''1999'''
*[[Račak massacre]], Rambouillet talks fail. NATO starts a military campaign in Kosovo and bombards FR Yugoslavia in [[1999 NATO bombing of the Federal Republic of Yugoslavia|Operation Allied Force]].<ref>{{cite news | date=24 March 1999 | publisher=CNN | url=http://edition.cnn.com/WORLD/europe/9903/24/kosovo.strikes/ | title=NATO attack on Yugoslavia begins | access-date=7 July 2017 | archive-date=13 April 2017 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170413140840/http://edition.cnn.com/WORLD/europe/9903/24/kosovo.strikes/ | url-status=dead }}</ref>
*Following Milošević's signing of an agreement, control of Kosovo is handed to the United Nations, but still remains a part of Yugoslavia's federation. After losing wars in Croatia, Bosnia and Kosovo, numerous Serbs leave those regions to find refuge in remainder of Serbia. In 1999, Serbia was host to some 700,000 Serb refugees or internally displaced.<ref name="Bleak outlook for Serb refugees">{{cite news|date=22 March 2000|work=BBC News|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/europe/687441.stm|title=Bleak outlook for Serb refugees|first=Jacky|last=Rowland|location=Belgrade|access-date=4 July 2012|archive-date=16 August 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210816074841/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/europe/687441.stm|url-status=live}}</ref>
*[[Preševo Valley Conflict|Fresh fighting]] erupts between Albanians and Yugoslav security forces in Albanian populated areas outside of Kosovo, with the intent of joining three municipalities to Kosovo ([[Preševo]], [[Bujanovac]] and [[Medveđa]]).
*[[Franjo Tuđman]] dies. Shortly after, his party loses the elections.

[[File:NATO damage in Belgrade.jpg|thumb|right|[[General Staff Building, Belgrade|General Staff Building]] in Belgrade, damaged during the [[1999 NATO bombing of the Federal Republic of Yugoslavia|1999 NATO bombing]]]]

'''2000'''
*[[Overthrow of Slobodan Milošević]]. [[Vojislav Koštunica]] becomes the new president of Yugoslavia. With Milošević ousted and a new government in place, FR Yugoslavia restores ties with the west. The political and economic sanctions are suspended in total, and FRY is reinstated in many political and economic organizations, as well as becoming a candidate for other collaborative efforts.

'''2001'''
*An Albanian [[2001 insurgency in Macedonia|insurgency]] begins in [[North Macedonia|Macedonia]].
*Conflict in Southern Serbia ends in defeat for Albanians.
*Conflict in Macedonia ends with the [[Ohrid Agreement (2001)|Ohrid Agreement]].

